    "turn_idx": 48,
    "turn_text": "power. Women rule. Now here's the thing, it's a decision. We made a decision a long time ago that we had to change the demographics of the Congress of the United States. When I came to Congress, out of 435 people, there were 12 Democratic women and 11 Republican women. We made a decision that every election we would add to our numbers. And now we have over, for the first time, over 100, 106 women were elected in the last election. 91 of them Democrats, because we made a decision. They went from 11 to 15, and we went from 12 to 91, because we made a decision. Nothing, again, is more wholesome than the political process and government. Actually, in everything you can name, but I'll stick with what I know, than the increased participation of women and women in the leadership. Now, in this new Congress, we have a most diverse Congress. Our caucus is 60% women, people of color LGBTQ. It's very exciting. And just to be all too young, but read about it in the history book. When the waterbait babies came to Washington, This was transformative. It was a big class. Similar to this, transformative big class. In the first year, not one of those freshmen was chair of a subcommittee, had a gavel. In this class, 18 freshmen have a gavel. 10 of them are women, eight of them are men. So it is really important to preside, to decide what you're going to take up and who you're going to recognize. power. We're just so proud because we're not only women to have a seat at the table, we want them to have a seat at the head of the table.",
